`_uncatalogued_external_modules` already excluded a module whose file the
analysis read (`_analyzed_modules`, path-derived) and one the language marks
internal (`is_definitionally_first_party`). Neither can see a repo that refers
to itself by the name it PUBLISHES, because that name lives in a manifest and
not in the directory layout. caddy is the sharp case: its relative spellings
(`modules/caddyhttp`) were already suppressed while
`github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/modules/caddyhttp` was reported -- the same
package judged two ways by how the importing file happened to spell it.

A third mechanism, `supply_chain.collect_first_party_package_names`, reads the
published name from Cargo.toml `[package].name`, go.mod `module`, and
package.json `name`; the CLI threads it to both coverage call sites the way
`higher_fidelity_available` already is.

THE ITEM'S PREMISE WAS REFUTED IN THE CODE FIRST. INV-vivok proposed keying on
`supply_chain_tier` and rejected it because the tier "is None on every package
node". It is not: ir.py declares `supply_chain_tier: int = 1` and serialises it
NESTED under `supply_chain.tier`, so only a top-level read returns None.
Measured over 42 cached surveys, 796 package nodes: 187 tier 1, 609 tier 3,
none None. The conclusion survives for a better reason -- a package node's tier
comes from its DECLARING FILE'S path, so a third-party package declared in an
in-repo manifest reads first_party (`cmake:CMakeLists.txt:204:"GnuTLS":package`
at tier 1). The manifest is read directly instead.

MEASURED on each repo's own cached survey, the instrument the item was filed
with: bellman 15 -> 14 (exactly `bellman.VerificationError`), caddy 158 -> 151
(the four entries the item named plus three it had not). Nothing added in
either arm. Corpus-wide over 42 surveys: 154 suppressed, 0 added, 10/42 repos
affected. Since suppression is the false-clean direction, each entry was
re-checked against its repo's declared dependencies: 90 collide and all 90 are
the workspace-sibling shape INV-nuzas already rules first-party; narrowing to
names declared only as a registry version leaves 4, in 2 repos, a monorepo that
publishes to npm and depends on itself by version -- disclosed in the
collector's docstring rather than asserted away.

Controls hold: bellman still reports blake2s_simd, rand, rayon, ff.Field;
caddy still reports all of Go's stdlib. The match is component-bounded so
`caddy` cannot swallow `caddyserver`, pinned by tests. As the item predicted,
no verdict moves -- 14 and 151 entries still withhold both repos.

Also corrects the same false `supply_chain_tier` claim where it had been
copied into test_first_party_module_grammar.py's docstring.

